在凌晨3点到4点之间。所以这是时间，你知道，月亮将在3点消失，太阳将在4点升起，这是你的时间。假设这将非常直接，但结果并非如此。

115
00:57:46.000 --> 00:58:07.000
So this is Private Waltos Stagles of the 3rd Australian Battalion. He was part of the second wave that went ashore. I'll read what he said. It was pitch dark, then all of a sudden the coast A dim outline of the coast loomed up. As we got closer, we were all beginning to get tensed up, nervous, wondering what was going to happen, as everything was so quiet.
这是第三澳大利亚营的沃尔托斯·斯泰格尔列兵。他是第二波登陆部队的一员。我来读读他所说的话。当时漆黑一片，突然间，海岸……海岸的轮廓隐约显现。当我们靠近时，大家都开始变得紧张、焦虑，不知道会发生什么，因为周围实在太安静了。

116
00:58:07.000 --> 00:58:39.000
Then a single shot rang out and a yellowish light flared up in the sky, and from then on the Turks let loose, machine gun and rifle fire at the boats. As soon as the boats grounded, it was every man for himself. And it's very saving private Ryan. So an army surgeon, Australian surgeon, wrote in his diary that he watched the men going ashore and draw this Turkish fire. Several fell as they ran and on the beach I saw even more men lying untidily. Some quite still, others making an occasional movement. Then I jumped over in a two feet of water and waded heavily ashore.
接着，一声枪响划破寂静，天空中亮起一道黄色的光，从那一刻起，土耳其人开始疯狂射击，机枪和步枪子弹倾泻向船只。船一靠岸，每个人都只能自求多福。这场景非常像《拯救大兵瑞恩》。一位澳大利亚军医在日记中写道，他看着士兵们上岸并吸引了土耳其人的火力。几个人在奔跑中倒下，我在海滩上看到更多人横七竖八地躺着。有的纹丝不动，有的偶尔抽动一下。然后我跳进两英尺深的水里，艰难地涉水上岸。

117
00:58:39.000 --> 00:58:55.000
The lapping edge was already pink and frothy with fallen men. Oh, God. So it takes them about four hours to get their men ashore the Anzax. That's amazing. They do get ashore, really. It is. It's very impressive. I mean, that opening reading, I mean, they do perform very, very bravely.
水边已经被倒下的士兵染成了粉红色，泛着泡沫。天哪。所以澳新军团花了大约四个小时才让士兵全部上岸。这太惊人了。他们确实上岸了。确实。这非常令人印象深刻。我的意思是，开篇读的那段，他们表现得非常、非常勇敢。

118
00:58:55.000 --> 00:59:26.000
Yeah. They start to push up the hill up inland, but it is clear that their guiding assumption was wrong. The Turks have not cut and run, quite the reverse. So this is another guy, Eric Moorhead from Victoria. He says basically they went up the hill, they're expecting to see the enemy there. We were now in a scrub-covered plane fairly in the open. No Turks would have be seen, but the air was literally full of bullets and the sound was deafening. The point-blank explosion of rifles and the concealed snipers firing on us at close quarters.
是的。他们开始向内陆的山上推进，但很明显，他们最初的假设是错误的。土耳其人并没有溃逃，恰恰相反。这是另一个人，来自维多利亚州的埃里克·穆尔黑德。他基本上说他们上了山，本以为会在那里看到敌人。我们现在处于一片灌木丛生的平原上，相当开阔。看不到土耳其人，但空气中确实充满了子弹，声音震耳欲聋。步枪的近距离射击声和隐蔽的狙击手在近距离向我们开火。

119
00:59:26.000 --> 00:59:55.000
Our bayonet charge had failed, the men became disorganised. Some runabout distractedly poking in the bushes for Turks. Others fell on their faces, nerve-wracked by the terrible fire. So what has happened is this. The Ottoman troops have let them come inland. And then at the point where the Turks might have panicked under the Australian onslaught, they have rallied, and this is thanks to somebody who already mentioned in this episode, their frontline commander, lieutenant colonel, Mustafa Kemmel.
我们的刺刀冲锋失败了，士兵们变得混乱。有些人心烦意乱地在灌木丛中乱窜寻找土耳其人。另一些人则因可怕的炮火而神经崩溃，趴在地上。事情是这样的。奥斯曼军队让他们进入了内陆。然后在土耳其人可能因澳大利亚人的猛攻而惊慌失措的时候，他们重整旗鼓了，这要归功于本集提到过的那个人，他们的前线指挥官，穆斯塔法·凯末尔中校。

120
00:59:55.000 --> 01:00:22.000
So up to this point, as we said, he's an obscure sort of nationalist army officer. Born in Salonica, he's a big drinker, he's very intense, very driven. But people haven't heard of him. And this is the moment where particularly within the army, he becomes a national hero. Because at 10 o'clock that morning, Mustafa Kemal is heading towards the front line. And he sees some of the Turkish troops retreating. And he says, why are you running away? Sir, the enemy, where, over there?
到目前为止，正如我们所说，他只是一个默默无闻的民族主义军官。出生于萨洛尼卡，是个酒鬼，非常强硬，非常有干劲。但人们还没听说过他。而这一刻，特别是在军队内部，他成为了民族英雄。因为那天早上10点，穆斯塔法·凯末尔正前往前线。他看到一些土耳其军队在撤退。他说，你们为什么要逃跑？长官，敌人，在哪里？在那边？

121
01:00:22.000 --> 01:00:54.000
and they point to this place that the Australians end up calling Battleship Hill about two miles inland, which is where the Australians have got to. And Mustafa Akemil comes out with this line that becomes part of Turkish nationalist mythology. He says to his troops, I don't order you to attack. I order you to die. By the time we're dead, other troops and other commanders will be ready to take our places. And amazingly, I mean, I would find that terrifying. probably would continue running away.
他们指向澳大利亚人最终称之为“战列舰山”的地方，位于内陆约两英里处，也就是澳大利亚人到达的地方。穆斯塔法·凯末尔说出了那句成为土耳其民族主义神话一部分的话。他对他的部队说，我不命令你们进攻，我命令你们去死。在我们死后，其他部队和其他指挥官会准备好接替我们的位置。令人惊讶的是，我的意思是，我会觉得那很可怕。我可能会继续逃跑。

122
01:00:54.000 --> 01:01:15.000
But the Turks are made of sterner stuff than I am. And they turn round and they run back into their fray. And they drive the Australians back down towards the beach. I mean, the Australians do love a beach. Australians do love a beach. But you wouldn't associate Australians for going backwards, would you? No, you wouldn't. But that is precisely what they are doing by mid-afternoon.
但土耳其人比我意志坚定得多。他们转过身，跑回了战场。他们把澳大利亚人赶回了海滩方向。我的意思是，澳大利亚人确实喜欢海滩。澳大利亚人确实喜欢海滩。但你不会把澳大利亚人与后退联系在一起，对吧？不，不会。但这就是他们到下午中期时正在做的事情。

123
01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:36.000
Far from going forwards and pushing inland, they're being driven back towards the sea. Their officers are being sithed down by Turkish machine guns. and they're absolutely shattered. Of course, I mean, imagine how exhausted they must be after making this landing. So this is Private Herbert Filders of the 12th Battalion. Four of us lay under shrapnel machine gun and rifle fire,
他们非但没有前进并向内陆推进，反而被赶回了大海。他们的军官被土耳其机枪扫射倒下。他们彻底崩溃了。当然，我的意思是，想象一下他们在完成这次登陆后该有多疲惫。这是第12营的赫伯特·菲尔德斯列兵。我们四个人躺在弹片、机枪和步枪火力的压制下，

124
01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:54.000
not daring to lift our heads the whole while. If we'd budged or would have been killed dozens of times over. The bullets were streaming so thick over our heads. Our officers said he'd never seen anything like it, and he's an old soldier. I was jolly tired, too. As a matter of fact, I went to sleep twice. That's impressive. That's so impressive.
全程不敢抬头。如果我们动一下，早就被杀了几十次了。子弹在我们头顶密集地飞过。我们的军官说他从未见过这样的场面，他可是一位老兵了。我也非常累。事实上，我睡着了两次。这令人印象深刻。这太令人印象深刻了。

125
01:01:54.000 --> 01:02:16.000
I mean, do you think, is this worse than fighting it on the Western Front? It is really, isn't it? Yeah, I think in the grand scheme of things. I mean, you're kind of in the open, aren't you? You don't know what you're doing? You're blundering around all these, your mad landscape. Yeah, it's really hot. As we will see, particularly in our next episode, in Thursday's episode.
我的意思是，你认为这比在西线作战更糟糕吗？确实是这样，不是吗？是的，我认为从大局来看。我的意思是，你有点暴露在开阔地带，不是吗？你不知道自己在做什么？你在所有这些疯狂的地形中跌跌撞撞。是的，真的很热。正如我们将看到的，特别是在我们的下一集，周四的那一集中。

126
01:02:16.000 --> 01:02:38.000
I think the conditions are Gallipoli are just unbelievably hellish. Yeah. And they're definitely worse than the Western Front. And a lot of people who served on both said, you know, the Western Front wasn't brilliant. But the one thing it had in its favour was it wasn't Gallipoli, which was so much worse. Yeah, it sounds awful. So anyway, this bloke who fell asleep. He was hit three times in the legs, but he still managed to get that to the beach, which is very impressive.
我认为加里波利的条件简直是地狱般的。是的。它们绝对比西线更糟糕。许多在两地都服役过的人说，你知道，西线并不好。但它唯一的好处是它不是加里波利，加里波利要糟糕得多。是的，听起来太可怕了。总之，这个睡着了的家伙。他的腿部中了三枪，但他还是设法到达了海滩，这非常令人印象深刻。

127
01:02:38.000 --> 01:03:14.000
Have a bobby. Anyway, night falls, the Anzaks do have a foothold. They've got this little pocket with a kind of perimeter, which is about two miles long. But in total, they've basically got only half a mile. in land. They are nowhere near where they were meant to be, and they've already lost 2,000 men out of their 16,000 killed, wounded or missing. So that's the Anzax. Now, meanwhile, the British have been landing further south at Cape Helles. And this is actually the bigger landing. This is 21,000 men of the 29th Division. And they're given a tremendous send-off by their
真是个硬汉。总之，夜幕降临，澳新军团确实站稳了脚跟。他们有了一个小据点，周围有一圈大约两英里长的防线。但总的来说，他们基本上只深入了半英里。他们离预定目标还差得很远，而且在1.6万人中已经损失了2000人，包括阵亡、受伤或失踪。这就是澳新军团。与此同时，英国人已经在更南边的海丽丝角登陆了。这实际上是规模更大的登陆。这是第29师的2.1万名士兵。他们受到了指挥官艾尔玛·亨特·韦斯顿少将的盛大欢送。

128
01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:33.000
commanding officer, Major General Ayelma Hunter Western. Hunter Bunta. He says to them, The eyes of the world are upon us. Your deeds will live in history. To us now is given the opportunity of avenging our friends and relatives who have died in France and Flanders. So I looked him up. Yeah. Moustache. Very good moustache.
亨特·邦塔。他对他们说，全世界的目光都在注视着我们。你们的事迹将载入史册。现在我们有机会为在法国和法兰德斯牺牲的朋友和亲人复仇。所以我查了一下他。是的。胡子。非常好的胡子。

129
01:03:33.000 --> 01:03:57.000
Excellent moustache. I think the best British military moustache I've seen so far. I think the moustaches at Gallipoli, by and large, are better than across the board than British moustaches on the Western Front. So Ataturk had an excellent moustache. It's a pencil, though. Hunter Buntus moustache. is so ect British military officer in the First World War. It's the platonic archetype of it.
出色的胡子。我认为这是我目前见过的最好的英国军人胡子。我认为加里波利的胡子总体上比西线的英国胡子要好。所以阿塔图尔克有一撇出色的胡子。不过那是撇细胡。亨特·邦塔的胡子是第一次世界大战中典型的英国军官胡子。它是它的柏拉图式原型。

130
01:03:57.000 --> 01:04:19.000
Do look at it. Do have a look. Anyone who's a fan of military moustaches. Right. Now, things here at Cape Helles do not go according to plan at all. So I mentioned there are five beaches and the main ones. We'll forget three of them. We'll just talk about two of them, W and V. So the first wave to land at W Beach are the first Lancashire Fusiliers.
一定要看看。一定要看一看。任何喜欢军人胡子的人。好了。现在，海丽丝角的情况完全没有按计划进行。我提到有五个海滩，主要的那些。我们忘了其中三个。我们只谈其中两个，W海滩和V海滩。第一波在W海滩登陆的是第一兰开夏燧发枪团。

131
01:04:19.000 --> 01:04:41.000
and when they get there they find that the Turks are waiting with all their barbed wire and their machine guns. I mean, this is Captain Harold Clayton. There was tremendously strong barbed wire when my boat landed. Men were being hit on the boats and as they splashed ashore. I got up to my waist and water. I tripped over a rock and went under. I got up and made for the shore and laid down by the barbed wire. The front of the wire by now is a thick mass of men,
当他们到达那里时，发现土耳其人带着所有的铁丝网和机枪在等着他们。这是哈罗德·克莱顿上尉。我的船登陆时，那里有非常坚固的铁丝网。士兵们在船上和涉水上岸时被击中。我水深及腰。我被一块石头绊倒，沉了下去。我站起来向岸边跑去，躺在铁丝网旁。铁丝网前现在是一大堆士兵，

132
01:04:41.000 --> 01:04:59.000
the majority of whom never moved again. The trenches on the right raped us and those above us raked our right, while trenches and machine guns fired straight down the valley. The noise was ghastly. The sight's horrible. I mean, just, so they've got inland. They're stuck on the barbed wire and they're just being absolutely raked with machine gun fire.
他们中的大多数人再也没有动过。右侧的战壕扫射我们，上方的人扫射我们的右侧，而战壕和机枪直接向山谷开火。噪音非常可怕。景象很恐怖。我的意思是，他们进入了内陆。他们被困在铁丝网上，正被机枪火力彻底扫射。

133
01:04:59.000 --> 01:05:26.000
So the Lancash Fusiliers, they went to shore with 27 officers and 1,002 men. They end the day with 16 officers and just 304 men. But have they secured a foothold? They have secured a little foothold. I mean, they definitely got ashore. So that's something. Now, if you think that's bad, V Beach is the real shocker. So V Beach, the Turks had really put a lot of effort into reinforcing this beach.
所以兰开夏燧发枪团，他们带着27名军官和1002名士兵上岸。一天结束时，只剩下16名军官和304名士兵。但他们守住据点了吗？他们确实守住了一个小据点。我的意思是，他们确实上岸了。所以这算是一点成就。现在，如果你认为这很糟糕，V海滩才是真正的噩梦。在V海滩，土耳其人确实花了很多精力来加固这个海滩。

134
01:05:26.000 --> 01:05:58.000
They had put two lines of barbed wire and then behind them to various lines of trenches. So one of the British naval commanders, who's called Edward Unwin, comes up with a clever plan that you and indeed Rupert Brooke would admire. He says, look, we're just across the straits from Troy. And, you know, if you've done classics at public school, as I have, then you will know how to cope with Trojans. We need a Trojan horse. Not a literal horse. Not a literal horse, but a ship.
他们设置了两道铁丝网，后面还有几道战壕。所以一位名叫爱德华·昂温的英国海军指挥官想出了一个你，甚至鲁珀特·布鲁克都会钦佩的聪明计划。他说，看，我们就在特洛伊对面的海峡。而且，如果你像我一样在公立学校学过古典文学，那么你就会知道如何对付特洛伊人。我们需要一匹特洛伊木马。不是真正的马。不是真正的马，而是一艘船。

135
01:05:58.000 --> 01:06:13.000
Roll the enormous horse on the beach. I mean, it would be original. Frankly. I mean, it couldn't go worse. I wouldn't put it past them. And you're not wrong. It couldn't go worse than what they actually did. So they said, instead of using a,
把那匹巨大的马滚到海滩上。我的意思是，那将是原创的。坦率地说。我的意思是，情况不会更糟了。我不会觉得他们做不出这种事。你没说错。情况确实不会比他们实际做的更糟。所以他们说，与其使用一匹木马，

136
01:06:13.000 --> 01:06:32.000
a horse, a wooden horse, we'll use a bloody big ship like a huge old collier, a coal ship called the SS River Clyde. We will put 2,000 men, we'll put loads of ammo, and we'll put loads of supplies in this big old ship, and then we will deliberately run it aground.
我们不如用一艘该死的大船，比如一艘巨大的旧运煤船，叫“克莱德河”号。我们将把2000名士兵、大量弹药和大量补给装进这艘大旧船里，然后我们故意让它搁浅。

137
01:06:32.000 --> 01:07:01.000
We'll run the ship aground as close to the beach as possible. We'll have another little ship coming up called a steamhopper, and this will go in between the ship and the actual sand. So all the men will be able to jump off the River Clyde onto the steamhopper and then jump off the steamhopper onto the sands and run up the beach. Brilliant. And then this big ship that's stuck on the beach, the River Clyde, we can use that as our headquarters, as a little field hospital,
我们会让船尽可能靠近海滩搁浅。我们会让另一艘小船过来，叫蒸汽驳船，它会在大船和沙滩之间穿梭。这样所有的士兵都能从“克莱德河”号跳到蒸汽驳船上，然后再从蒸汽驳船跳到沙滩上，跑上岸。太棒了。然后这艘搁浅在海滩上的大船，“克莱德河”号，我们可以把它用作我们的总部，作为一个小型野战医院，

138
01:07:01.000 --> 01:07:20.000
as a kind of supply depot, all of this. I think it's quite a good idea. Do you? How does it go? Well, we'll find out. So they start bombarding the beach. and then they're going to send up the Dublin fusiliers first and little rowing boats and then this bloody big ship, the River Clyde.
作为一个补给站，等等。我觉得这是个好主意。你觉得呢？结果如何？嗯，我们会知道的。所以他们开始轰炸海滩，然后他们打算先派都柏林燧发枪团乘坐小划艇上去，然后是这艘该死的大船，“克莱德河”号。

139
01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:41.000
The Dublin fuseliers make very slow progress, so unfortunately they land out of order. The River Clyde lands first or crashes a ground, but it hasn't got as close to the beach as they hoped it would. It's still 80 yards away. So the men can't jump out and wade. They will just drown. So this is where your steam hopper little boat, which would act as a bridge.
都柏林燧发枪团进展非常缓慢，所以不幸的是他们登陆的顺序乱了。“克莱德河”号先登陆或搁浅了，但它没有像他们希望的那样靠近海滩。它还在80码外。所以士兵们不能跳下去涉水。他们会淹死的。这就是你的蒸汽驳船小船作为桥梁发挥作用的地方。

140
01:07:41.000 --> 01:08:02.000
kind of a gangway comes in. Unfortunately, the steam hopper has also run aground, but on the wrong side of the ship and pointing the wrong way. So they can't use that at all. So they're all stuck on this boat offshore. Like how are we going to get off? At this point, the Dublin fusiliers and their rowing boats come into land, and they are absolutely sitting ducks for the Turkish machine gunners
某种舷梯出现了。不幸的是，蒸汽驳船也搁浅了，但在船的错误一侧，而且方向不对。所以他们根本无法使用它。所以他们都被困在离岸的船上。就像我们怎么下去？此时，都柏林燧发枪团和他们的划艇进入登陆，他们完全成了土耳其机枪手的活靶子，

141
01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:23.000
who basically sigh through them. Some of the Dublin blokes jump out and they try to wade ashore, but their packs are so heavy that, they can't really make any progress. They end up being riddled with Turkish bullets. It's a terrible, terrible scene. Private Robert Martin. There were 25 in my boat and there were only three of us left.
基本上被扫射。一些都柏林人跳出来试图涉水上岸，但他们的背包太重了，他们无法真正前进。他们最终被土耳其子弹打成了筛子。这是一个非常、非常可怕的场景。罗伯特·马丁列兵。我的船上有25个人，只剩下我们三个了。

142
01:08:23.000 --> 01:08:41.000
It was terrible to hear our poor chums moaning and to see the poor boys dead in the water. Others on the beach roaring for help, but we could do nothing for them. Those who were left wounded on the shore. In the evening, the tide came in and they were all drowned. And I was left by myself on the beach. So that's not great.
听到我们可怜的伙伴们的呻吟，看到可怜的孩子们死在水里，真是太可怕了。海滩上的其他人大声呼救，但我们无能为力。那些受伤留在岸上的人。晚上，潮水涨了，他们都被淹死了。我独自留在海滩上。所以这不太好。

